{
  // tsconfig.lwe-ask.json — the LWE ask Worker at lwe-ask/.
  //
  // ONE PROGRAM PER AUXILIARY WORKER, for the reason tsconfig.tools.json's
  // header gives about tools/: a program is only as accurate as the globals it
  // declares. These three are separately deployed Workers with their own
  // wrangler.toml and their own dependency sets, so they get Cloudflare's
  // globals and nothing else.
  //
  // The three src trees were the last runtime code in this repo that no tsc
  // program held. They reach production, and nothing checked them, which is the
  // orphan case check-tool-types.mjs's floor exists to catch one directory over.
  //
  // TESTS ARE DELIBERATELY OUT. lwe-ask has none; the two that exist next door
  // import their Worker's source, so a program carrying node globals for the
  // test pulls Worker modules in transitively and checks them against the wrong
  // environment. Measured 2026-08-21 with `types: ["bun"]` over both test files:
  // 5 of the 11 diagnostics were that artifact (`cf` does not exist in
  // RequestInit, `RequestInitCfProperties` not found, `innerHTML` on Node), all
  // clean in the program whose globals match. Covering the tests wants the
  // diagnostic filtering check-tool-types.mjs already implements, which is its
  // own piece of work rather than a wider `include` here.
  //
  // `strict` is false to match every other program in config/. Raise it per
  // directory once the existing tail is paid down.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "target": "esnext",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    // Workers globals only. With lib.dom loaded, its CacheStorage shadows
    // Cloudflare's, which is the collision tsconfig.json's header documents.
    "lib": ["esnext"],
    // index.ts imports its two sibling modules by their real `.ts` filenames,
    // which is the one specifier that resolves in both trees (tsconfig.json's
    // header has the long version). Without this tsc rejects them outright and
    // never reaches a real finding.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    // Empty rather than omitted (an omitted `types` admits every @types/*, bun's
    // included); the Workers runtime comes in through `include`, generated by
    // tools/gen-runtime-types.ts from the pinned workerd. See tsconfig.json.
    "types": [],
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // Two control-flow checks, set here because they are set in tsconfig.json
    // and a program is only as strict as its own options. Both cost nothing:
    // +0 diagnostics on every one of the ten programs, measured 2026-08-27.
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    // strictNullChecks ON. `strict` stays off: it bundles noImplicitAny,
    // which is 7,197 diagnostics of annotation labour across this repo, and
    // raising the two together buries the checks that find bugs under the
    // ones that find missing annotations. This program is clean under it,
    // and a contract test keeps the set of clean programs from shrinking.
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  // A GLOB rather than a file list, so a new module joins the program by
  // existing. The allowlist habit is what let ten client islands go unchecked.
  "include": ["../lwe-ask/src/**/*.ts", "./.generated/workers-runtime.d.ts"]
}
